[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky and GM Alex Colovic videos"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]
[SourceTitle "Master Class | Queen's Gambit | Chess Speedrun | Grandmaster Naroditsky; Queen's Gambit Declined explained by GM Alex Colovic"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DCBwXvH3kH4"]
[SecondarySourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4FkLzafa8YY"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 Nf6
3. c4 e6
4. Nc3 Be7
5. Bf4 O-O
6. e3 a6
7. Bd3
{This is the reference move from the weak deck. Naroditsky repeatedly points out that `Bd3` is the accurate developing square in Queen's Gambit structures because it blocks annoying queen-file tactics and supports kingside play.}
(7. cxd5
{This was the old weak move. It is playable, but if you exchange early you must know the resulting structure instead of drifting.}
7... exd5
8. h3 c5
9. dxc5 Bxc5
10. Be2
{White has not lost, but Black has easy development. Prefer `Bd3` first unless you specifically want this structure.})
7... c5
{Black challenges the center.}
8. O-O
{Finish development before clarifying the center.}
8... dxc4
9. Bxc4 b5
10. Bd3 Bb7
11. Rc1 Nbd7
12. Qe2
{White's plan is simple: complete development, keep the bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal, and meet later `...cxd4` with either exd4 IQP play or piece pressure on the c-file.}

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - QGD c5 Exchange Reminder"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DCBwXvH3kH4"]
[Date "2021.08.08"]
[Round "2"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "QGD c5"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]

1. Nf3 Nf6
2. d4 d5
3. c4 e6
4. Nc3 Be7
5. Bf4 O-O
6. e3 c5
7. cxd5
{This matches the weak deck's study point. If Black breaks immediately with `...c5`, exchanging is fine, but the lesson is structural: know whether you are playing against an isolated pawn, a Carlsbad structure, or a free-piece-development position.}
7... Nxd5
8. Nxd5 exd5
9. Bd3
{Back to Naroditsky's recurring prescription: put the bishop on d3 and aim at h7 before Black is fully coordinated.}
9... cxd4
10. exd4 Nc6
11. O-O Bg4
12. Be2
{Do not panic about the pin. Break it, then use the open files and the d4-pawn's space to develop active rooks.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Baltic and Pseudo-Slav"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DCBwXvH3kH4"]
[Date "2021.08.08"]
[Round "3"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Baltic / Pseudo-Slav"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video, applied to weak-deck sideline"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Baltic Defense, Pseudo-Slav"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 Bf5
3. c4 e6
4. Nc3 c6
5. Qb3
{Against an early outside bishop, make Black defend b7 and spend queen tempi.}
5... Qb6
6. c5
{Gain space and make the bishop on f5 justify itself.}
6... Qc7
7. Bf4 Qc8
8. Nh4
{This is the engine/reference move from the weak deck: ask the bishop whether it has a stable square.}
(8. e3
{The quieter weak-deck branch. It is playable, but lets Black breathe more easily.}
8... Nf6
9. Be2 Be7
10. O-O O-O
{If you choose `e3`, play it as a normal development line and do not forget that `Nh4` is often available later.})
8... Bg6
9. Nxg6 hxg6
10. e3 Nd7
11. Bd3
{White has won the bishop pair question: Black's light bishop has been traded or driven awkwardly, while White has a healthy center and can prepare e4.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Bg5 h6 Bh4 Structures"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4FkLzafa8YY"]
[Date "2022.05.19"]
[Round "4"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Semi-Slav / QGD"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Alex Colovic and GM Daniel Naroditsky videos"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]

1. Nf3 d5
2. d4 c6
3. c4 Nf6
4. Nc3 e6
5. Bg5 Nbd7
6. e3 Be7
7. Qc2 h6
8. Bh4
{This is the weak-deck study move. Preserve the pin and keep Black thinking about `...g5` weaknesses before deciding whether to take on f6.}
8... O-O
9. Rd1
{Bring a rook to the file before the center opens.}
9... Re8
10. Bd3 dxc4
11. Bxc4 b5
12. Bd3 Bb7
13. O-O
{Typical plan: develop calmly, answer `...h6` by retreating to h4, and only resolve the bishop tension when it wins time or damages Black's structure.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hX6W4X_CqkA"]
[Date "2021.10.19"]
[Round "5"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[SourceTitle "Master Class | Queen's Gambit Accepted | Chess Speedrun | Grandmaster Naroditsky"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hX6W4X_CqkA"]

1. Nf3 Nf6
2. d4 d5
3. c4 dxc4
4. e3
{The weak deck uses the solid recovery move. Naroditsky notes that the sharper `e4` is ambitious, but `e3` keeps d4 protected and gets the pawn back cleanly.}
(4. e4
{Ambitious Naroditsky-style alternative: build the big center and make Black attack it accurately.}
4... e6
5. Bxc4 c5
6. O-O cxd4
7. e5
{If you choose this route, remember his rule: recover the pawn and fight for d5.})
4... c5
5. Bxc4
{Recover the pawn.}
5... e6
(5... cxd4
6. exd4
{This is the weak-deck IQP structure. White accepts an isolated d-pawn in return for open lines and piece activity.}
6... e6
7. O-O Be7
8. Nc3 O-O
9. Qe2
{Use the IQP actively: Re1, Rd1, Bg5/Bf4, and sometimes d5 if Black lets you.})
6. O-O a6
7. a4
{The other weak-deck reference move. Stop `...b5` and keep the bishop from being kicked around for free.}
7... Nc6
8. Nc3 Be7
9. Qe2 O-O
10. Rd1 Qc7
{QGA summary: regain c4, develop fast, fight for the d5 square, and use `a4` against `...a6` so Black does not get easy queenside expansion.} *
